The following directory contains tools for helping allocate values for 
identifiers in the OASIS PKCS #11 spec. Under the current TC policy, these 
identifier values are allocated by the TC co-chairs and handed off to the 
proposers when their spec nears the time for TC balloting.

These are only tools to help the co-chairs manage allocating identifier values
for proposals. The official identifier values can be found in the balloted
proposals on the OASIS PKCS #11 page.

The tools in this directory include:
1) sample.prop - a sample proposal
2) ./process_proposal.pl - perl script to allocate idenifiers for a proposal.
3) ./approve_proposal.pl - perl script to move proposed mechanisms to approved mechanisms
4) ./release.pl - perl script to move approved identifiers to spec identifies.
5) ./verify.pl - perl script to verify the integrity of the database.
6) raw_ids.db - database of allocated identifiers.

-----------------------------------------------------------------

How to allocate identifiers for a new proposal, do this when you
are reasonably confident that you have the appropriate name and
number of identifier.

To figure out what identifiers you need to add, checkout the
proposal branch on your fork, got to working/identifier.db
and run ./parse_doc.pl and identifiers missing, you need to
add to your proposal. You do not need to add data structures
and typedefs to your proposal:

Make sure your fork is up to date, use git pull to update
the local copy of your branch, create a new branch specifically
for In a new, *UP TO DATE* branch:

1)  Create a new proposal. Proposals have:
   1.1) first line is the proposal name. Your proposals should be in the pkcs11vX.X
   directory where X.X is the current release.
   1.2) type of identifier. you can list the types with:
           $ ./verify.pl types
   1.3) list of identifiers and proposed values of the form:
           #define <identifier> <value>
    value must be a hex value. If value is TBD, then a new identifer value
    will be allocated. For mechanisms, adding the 'opt' option will allocate
    from one of the low number gaps, otherwise it will allocate based on the
    larget currently allocated.
   1.4) optionally next type of identifier (say for example, new mypqalg param set),
    followed by it's list. (now paramsets are each in their own space).
    Blank lines are ignored.

2) run ./process_proposal.pl as follows:
	$ ./process_proposal.pl pkcs11vX.X/myproposal.prop
3) git add raw_ids.db pkcs11vX.x/myproposal.prop pkcs11vX.X/myproposal.result
4) git commit
5) git push (to create a pull request).
6) optionally ask for a reviewer, your pull request will be voted on in the
   next meeting.

   This will create identifiers in the proposed state. Once they are merged,
   New identifiers can be allocated, but they will not show up in the scanners
   until they are marked approved.
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Once the pull request has been approved, 

1)update your fork
2) git checkout master; git pull (to make your master current)
3) Rebase your proposal branch to your master. The new identifier db
should now be in your proposal branch.
4) run:
./approve_proposal.pl pkcs11vX.X/myproposal.result (generated above)
5) run
./verify.pl header
This will tell you the new approved identifiers. If you did all the steps
right, it should display the same identifiers as ./parse_doc.pl
6) edit ../headers/pkcs11t.h to add these identifiers and the parameters
from your spec. This will keep the header file and doc in sync.
7) run
./verify.pl header, and
./parse_doc.pl 
to make sure everything is in sync.
8) commit your changes, the push to update your pull request.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
How to examine the database:

./verify.pl will check the database for invalid and duplicate entries.
In addition, you can supply any of the following keywords:

help: get a list of the commands.
dump: displays the entire database values.
types: list the valid types currently in the database as well as the next
  unallocated identifer, or the unallocated flag bits.
<type> list all identifiers of type <type>
<dispositon> list all identifiers of a particular dispostion (proposed, approved, spec) ect.
header [full] [path]: verify that the pkcs11t.h header is consistant with the
database. If full is specified, then it expects identifiers that are 
disposition=proposed to be in the header, othersize './verify.pl header' checks
that only approved and spec are in the header. The output include:
 - Entries that are in the database, but missing from the header.
 - Entries that are in both the database and the header, but their value
   is inconsistant.
  - Entries that are in the header, but not in the database. In this last
   case there are some special entries that help reduce the noise of false
   positives. 1) the aliases.db contain a list of entries that are deprecated
   in favor of the 'official' name. The aliases may show up in the header.
   2) Entries of the form CK?_VENDOR_DEFINED and have the value 0x80000000UL
   are ignored.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
How to lookup an identifier in the database:

./lookup identifier

if identifier is a type or disposition, it does the same as ./verify.pl.
identifier could also be a hex value, in which case it does a reverse lookup.
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Format of raw_ids.db:

each line is a record.
{identifier},{value},{type},{disposition}

{identifier} is the PKCS #11 #define value (example: CKM_AES_CBC).
{value} is the value of the identifier in hex.
{type} is the type of identifier. Multiple identifiers with the same type and value is prohibited. Valid types can be listed with:
    $ ./verify.pl types
{dispositon} what is the state of this identifier value pair:
    spec - identifer is in an approved OASIS PKCS #11 spec
    proposed - identifer has been allocated for a proposal
    approved - the proposal with this identifer has been approved by the TC.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

How to verify the header file against the spec:

./parse_doc.pl [commands...] [header_file] [doc_directories ... ]
parse one or more markdown documents and/or a header file and output
according to the command:
    base={base_dir}:  location of the root of the working tree, default is ../
    verbose:  output inconsistancies in the document
    verbose2:  output objects in the header_file that are not in the doc_file
    no_doc_errors:  only print a summary of missing document values
    table:  the first two columns of every table in the document(s)
    defines:  all the defines in both the document(s)and header_file
    typedefs: all the typedefs in both the document(s) and header_file
    structs:  all the structs in both the documents(s) and header_file
    doc_defines:     all the defines in the document(s)
    doc_typedefs:    all the typedefs in the document(s)
    doc_structs:     all the structs in the document(s)
    doc_all:         all the parsed values in the document(s)
    header_defines:  all the defines in the header_file
    header_typedefs: all the typedefs in the header_file
    header_structs:  all the structs in the header_file
    header_all:      all the parsed values in the document(s)
    diff_defines:  the differences betweeen the defines in the document(s) and the header_file
    diff_typedefs: the differences between the typedefs in document(s) and header_file
    diff_structs:  the differences between the structs in document(s) and the header_file
    diff_all:  all the diffs
1. documents are specified by the directory. files.txt or
   spec_files.txt are read to find the individual files of the doc.
2. if more than one document directory is specified all directories
   are read and treated as one combined document. Adding profile and
   historical directories will turn on scanning of those defines
   in the header.
3. if documents and the header is required, the header_file must be first.
4. if no commands are supplied, then diff_all is assumed.

Between ./verify.pl and ./parse_doc.pl it's possible to make sure all the
defines in the document have been allocated, all the defines that are allocated
or in the document, and the headers are up to date with both.



